    id: "who-we-are",
    heading: "Who we are, and what working with a team in India means for a German company",
    paragraphs: [
      "BtechWaleTech consists of three engineers in India. Ankur Kumar is a full stack developer responsible for front ends, back ends and integrations. Santosh Sharma covers AI and machine learning, AWS, data, technical SEO and deployment. Vedansh Shrivastava leads project delivery and works on data science, AI and automation. A German client speaks to these three people from the first call to the final handover. There is no sales layer, no project manager relaying messages, and no silent subcontractor.",
      "It is worth being explicit about who we are. We are three freelance engineers working from India. We have no office in Germany, no GmbH, no German address and no German-speaking staff. Some offshore providers present themselves with a Berlin virtual office or a German phone number that forwards abroad; we think German buyers deserve to know exactly who they are contracting with. You would be hiring a small remote team in India, and this page describes honestly how that works, including where it fits and where it does not.",
      "German companies tend to value what remote work needs most: clear written specifications, agreed milestones, documented decisions and predictable delivery. We work that way by default. Every project starts with a scope document, the equivalent of a compact Pflichtenheft, listing pages, functions, interfaces, languages, responsibilities for content and legal texts, and what is out of scope. You approve it before anything is invoiced. During the build you get a password-protected staging environment and a shared list of feedback items, each with a status.",
