    id: "ecommerce-germany",
    heading: "Online shops in Germany: Stripe, Klarna, PayPal, SEPA, Shopware and Shopify",
    paragraphs: [
      "Germany is one of Europe's largest ecommerce markets and one of its most demanding. Customers are price-aware and careful, return rates in categories such as fashion are high, trust signals matter, and the legal framework for online selling is detailed. A shop that ignores German payment habits or legal requirements loses sales and, worse, can attract costly Abmahnungen, the formal warning letters competitors and associations send for legal errors.",
      "<strong>Payment methods.</strong> German shoppers are notably cautious about paying before they receive goods. PayPal is widely used, and purchase on invoice, Kauf auf Rechnung, remains popular, usually offered through a provider such as Klarna that takes the credit risk. SEPA direct debit is common for subscriptions and repeat purchases. Cards, Apple Pay and Google Pay complete the mix. Stripe can provide cards, wallets, SEPA direct debit and Klarna in a single integration; Shopify merchants can use Shopify Payments and PayPal; Shopware integrates PayPal, Mollie, Stripe and others through plugins. Sofort has been folded into Klarna's payment options, and giropay was discontinued at the end of 2024. Its successor from the European Payments Initiative, Wero, is being rolled out for online payments, and we check at build time which of your providers support it. Every merchant account is opened in your company's name.",
      "<strong>Choosing a platform.</strong> Shopify is the fastest route for most small and mid-sized D2C brands: hosting, security and updates are managed, and the app ecosystem covers German needs such as legal text plugins and invoice payment. Shopware is a German platform with strong B2B features, customer-specific pricing, rule-based promotions and a large German plugin ecosystem, and it can be self-hosted in Germany, which some companies prefer for data protection reasons. WooCommerce suits businesses already on WordPress. Headless builds suit high-traffic brands and complex catalogues that need maximum speed and a custom front end. We recommend one in the quote, with reasons.",
      "<strong>Legal elements in the checkout.</strong> German and EU consumer law shape the shop in concrete ways. Prices shown to consumers include VAT, with shipping costs clearly stated before checkout; products sold by weight, volume or length need a unit price; the order button must make clear that the order is binding and payable, for example with the wording zahlungspflichtig bestellen; customers need the withdrawal information and model withdrawal form for their 14-day right of withdrawal; and terms and conditions, privacy policy and Impressum must be reachable. Most merchants use legally reviewed texts from a specialised service or a lawyer and update them when the law changes; we integrate those texts and the checkout elements correctly. We are engineers, not lawyers, so the legal content itself is your adviser's responsibility.",
      "<strong>Shipping and returns.</strong> DHL, Hermes, DPD, GLS and UPS are the usual carriers, with Packstation and parcel shop delivery as popular options. We connect the shop to your shipping tool or carrier accounts so labels, tracking emails and return labels are generated automatically. For categories with frequent returns, a self-service returns flow saves customer service time and gives you data about why items come back.",
      "<strong>ERP and merchandise management.</strong> Many German merchants run JTL-Wawi, Xentral, plentymarkets, weclapp or an older ERP. Connecting the shop so orders, stock levels and invoices flow both ways is often more valuable than any design feature. We scope these interfaces carefully, using official APIs or established connectors, and test them with real order scenarios before launch.",
      "<strong>Marketplaces.</strong> Amazon.de, Otto and eBay are important sales channels in Germany. Your own shop remains where you own the customer relationship and margin. We can keep product data consistent across channels and design the own-shop experience around what marketplaces cannot offer: full product information, bundles, customer accounts with order history and B2B pricing.",
