  {
    id: "seo-germany",
    heading: "SEO for google.de: German-language search and hreflang for de-DE and English",
    paragraphs: [
      "Google holds the large majority of search in Germany, and German searchers write longer, more specific queries in many B2B fields. Good German SEO depends on three things: a technically clean site, a content structure that matches how German buyers phrase their needs, and German text that reads naturally. We handle the first two directly and work with your native speakers on the third.",
      "<strong>Technical SEO.</strong> Every build ships with clean URLs, unique titles and meta descriptions, structured data for organisation, products, services and FAQs, XML sitemaps, correct canonicals and redirects for any changed URLs. We connect Google Search Console and check indexing actively. For relaunches, a Relaunch in German marketing jargon, we export every URL that currently receives traffic or links and map each one to its new address, because broken URLs are the main cause of lost rankings after a new website goes live.",
      "<strong>German keyword structure.</strong> German forms compound nouns, so one concept can appear as a single long word or as separate words: Webdesign Agentur and Webdesignagentur, Edelstahl Rohr and Edelstahlrohr. Singular and plural, regional terms and English loanwords all compete. Many industrial buyers search with precise technical terms, standards and material grades. We build the keyword map from search data, your Search Console queries and your sales team's vocabulary, then structure pages so each one targets a distinct intent rather than several pages competing for the same term.",
      "<strong>Hreflang for DACH and English.</strong> If you serve Germany, Austria and Switzerland, or also sell internationally in English, the language setup matters. We use separate URLs per language and region, hreflang annotations such as de-DE, de-AT, de-CH and en, a sensible x-default, and a visible language switcher that does not redirect visitors automatically based on IP address. Austrian and Swiss variants only make sense where the content really differs, for example in prices, shipping, legal information or vocabulary. Otherwise a single German version targeted at de is often better.",
      "<strong>Form of address and tone.</strong> Whether a site addresses readers with Sie or du is a brand decision that affects every page, button and email. Most B2B and professional services use Sie; many consumer brands and startups use du. We ask this early so templates, form labels and system emails are consistent.",
      "<strong>Local search.</strong> For businesses serving specific cities, local pages with real substance, such as service areas, response times and completed projects, work much better than lists of city names. Your Google Business Profile must be owned and verified by you at your real German address; we guide the setup and categories but never create profiles for addresses you do not operate from.",
