  {
    id: "compliance",
    heading: "GDPR, TTDSG cookie consent, Impressum, BFSG accessibility and VAT",
    paragraphs: [
      "German websites face some of the strictest rules in Europe and an active warning-letter culture that enforces them. The points below describe how we build to reduce risk. This is not legal or tax advice: we are engineers, not lawyers, and your legal texts and compliance decisions should be reviewed by a qualified adviser in Germany.",
      "<strong>GDPR (DSGVO) in the build.</strong> Forms collect only the data needed for their purpose and link to the privacy policy. Nothing is pre-ticked. Form submissions are stored in access-controlled systems within the EU where possible, not forwarded to unknown third-party services. Admin areas use strong authentication and role-based access. Server logs are kept only as long as needed. We document the processors involved, such as the hosting provider, email service and analytics tool, so that your privacy policy and records of processing can list them accurately.",
      "<strong>Data processing agreement and transfers to India.</strong> Where our work gives us access to personal data, for example when maintaining a shop with customer accounts, we sign an Auftragsverarbeitungsvertrag under Article 28 GDPR. India does not have an adequacy decision from the European Commission, so the transfer is covered by the EU Standard Contractual Clauses, supported by technical measures. In practice we minimise access: development happens with test data, production access is limited to what a specific task needs, and personal data is not copied to our machines.",
      "<strong>Cookie consent under the TTDSG.</strong> Section 25 of the Telecommunications Telemedia Data Protection Act, renamed in 2024 as the TDDDG, requires consent before storing or reading information on a visitor's device unless it is strictly necessary for the service. That covers most analytics, marketing pixels, embedded videos and maps. We implement a consent management tool that blocks such scripts until consent is given, offers accept and reject with equal prominence, and records consent. Where you only need basic statistics, we recommend a cookieless analytics setup hosted in the EU, which often avoids the banner question for analytics altogether.",
      "<strong>Third-party embeds and fonts.</strong> German courts have treated loading Google Fonts from Google's servers without consent as a data protection violation, which triggered a wave of warning letters in 2022. We self-host all fonts, load YouTube videos and Google Maps only after a click with a notice, and avoid third-party scripts that are not necessary.",
      "<strong>Impressum.</strong> Commercial websites in Germany need a provider identification, the Impressum, now required under section 5 of the Digital Services Act implementation law, the DDG, which replaced the old section 5 TMG in 2024. It typically includes the company name and legal form, a serviceable address, legal representatives, contact details including email, register court and registration number, and VAT identification number where applicable. We build the page and link it from every page so it is reachable in no more than two clicks; you supply the content and have it checked.",
      "<strong>BFSG and the European Accessibility Act.</strong> The Barrierefreiheitsstärkungsgesetz transposes the European Accessibility Act and has applied since 28 June 2025. It covers certain products and services for consumers, including many ecommerce services, while micro-enterprises providing services are exempt. The harmonised European standard for digital accessibility refers to WCAG 2.1 level AA. We build to that level in practice: semantic HTML, keyboard operation, visible focus, sufficient contrast, text alternatives, labelled form fields, accessible error messages and an accessibility statement page where required. Whether and how the law applies to your company is a legal question for your adviser.",
      "<strong>VAT and our invoices.</strong> The German standard VAT rate is 19 percent, with a reduced rate of 7 percent for certain goods. For B2B services we supply from India to a German business, the place of supply is generally Germany and the reverse charge applies, meaning our invoice shows no German VAT and your company accounts for it in its return. We include your VAT ID on the invoice. Your shop's own VAT settings, including EU one-stop-shop rules for cross-border consumer sales, should be confirmed by your Steuerberater; we configure the shop accordingly.",
